4 CONC ine & Delaware); Thursday, January 5, 1995, Section MD Page 2:  "'MAC' MCMONAGLE; WAS TAVERN OWNER Francis J. "Mac" McMonagle, 95, of Wynnewood, a retired tavern owner, died Sunday at the Chateau Nurs
4 CONC ing and Rehabilitation Center in Bryn Mawr.   For 40 years, until retiring in 1975, Mr. McMonagle owned and operated Mac's Grille, a bar and restaurant at 63d Street and Lancaster Avenue in the Overbr
4 CONC ook section of Philadelphia.   He began a career in the food industry during the Depression in the early 1930s when he and his late mother baked pies and he delivered them to customers by trolley car,
4 CONC  said his daughter, Ann Callaghan. That led him to open a restaurant, the Golden Arrow, 54th Street and City Avenue, Philadelphia, and then Mac's Grille in 1935.   Mr. McMonagle, a native of Wilkes-Ba
4 CONC rre, served in the Marines during World War I.   After the war, he moved to Philadelphia with his family. He was a member of Presentation Blessed Virgin Mary Roman Catholic Church in Penn Wynne.  His 
4 CONC wife of 22 years, Helen B. Reilly McMonagle, died in 1944.   Besides his daughter, he is survived by two other children, Helene M. Addis and Mildred M. Clark; 28 grandchildren; and 56 great-grandchild
4 CONC ren.   A viewing will be held at 10:30 a.m. today at Presentation Blessed Virgin Mary Church on Haverford Road in Penn Wynne. A Mass of Christian Burial will follow at 11 a.m.   Burial will be in Holy
4 CONC  Sepulchre Cemetery in Cheltenham Township. Memorial contributions may be sent to Presentation Blessed Virgin Mary Church, 204 Haverford Rd., Wynnewood, Pa. 19096. Arrangements were made by the Stretc
4 CONC h Funeral Home in Havertown."
